            <ArticleTitle>Delayed recovery of left ventricular function after recanalization of a chronic coronary occlusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>491-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An unusually prolonged course of recovery of severely impaired left ventricular (LV) function (EF 32%) was observed in a patient after recanalization of a chronically occluded LAD. Despite persistent vessel patency, LV function remained depressed for almost 2 years after the percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty until complete recovery (EF 82%) could be observed. The possibility of a delayed recovery should be considered when assessing new therapeutic strategies to improve LV function of chronically ischemic myocardium.</AbstractText>
